      "body": "## Overview\n\nOn H100, Gemma-4 with FA4 JIT-compiles `FlashAttentionForwardSm90` on its first requests, about 4 s each. The SM90 dense warmup only builds the statically persistent kernel. This PR also warms the non-persistent prefill variant for the hd512 full-attention and hd256 sliding-window layers. Related: #49349, #63279.\n\n## Claims\n\n- No runtime FA4 JIT on Gemma-4 SM90 (2 per rank before).\n- First short request: 4.2 s → 0.28 s. First tool-call request: 3.9 s → 0.05 s.\n- Blackwell keys and the SM90 SplitKV/combine keys are unchanged.\n\n## Validation\n\nGemma-4 31B BF16 + MTP (k=4), TP8, H100, `--attention-config '{\"flash_attn_version\": 4}'`, `--jit-monitor-verbose`, with mixed sampling/JSON/tool traffic at batch sizes 1–64:\n\n| | FA4 warmup keys | Runtime FA4 JIT | 1st request | 1st tool call |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `main` | 5 | 2 per rank | 4.20 s | 4.02 s |\n| This PR | 8 | 0 | 0.28 s | 0.05 s |\n\nNo unit test yet, and no Hopper CI step covers this path.\n\n## Details\n\nFA4 only runs the persistent kernel when the packed query fits in one Q tile. Warmup compiles with `max_seqlen_q=1`. The V2 warmup's long-query runs all use more than 256 requests, which selects a different kernel variant (the varlen batch lookup). A small batch with a prompt longer than 16 tokens (hd512) or 64 tokens (hd256) therefore hit an uncompiled kernel. The SM90 hd256 sliding layers had no warmup keys at all.\n\nThe fix adds `is_static_persistent` to the compile key. Non-split keys compile with `max_seqlen_q=257`, which exceeds every SM90 tile. Warmup goes from 5 to 8 compiled kernels, still only for Gemma-4 with FA4.\n\n#63279 makes a similar change for Blackwell only and leaves SM90 as is. Both PRs edit the same function, so whichever lands second needs a rebase.\n\nAI assistance was used. I reviewed every line and validated the change as shown above.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>",
